We investigate the impact of rare decays involving a bottom qu transforming into a up quark and a lepton pair, specifically excluding charm quarks, which are influenced by neutrino branching fractions and lepton polarization. By utilizing the sensitivities of the LHC, we can constrain certain coupling combinations with a precision of around 10-20%. These constraints are somewhat relaxed due to final-state phase space differences, but can be strengthened by considering both tau and charmless decays with similar validity within the Standard Model. Comparing tau and muon branching ratios in charmless decays at the level of a few percent provides a powerful inelastic flavor universality test, with a sensitivity of around 1% enabled by recent experimental advances and accounting for theoretical uncertainties. Additionally, we can derive constraints on four-fermion couplings in the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
